# Standard legal terminology (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-09-17), verbatim:

> **Hard Rule: Do not ever make up your own terminology - you must
> always use standard legal contract terminology.**

## The rule

> **Draft partner and customer agreements only in standard legal
> terms, or in phrases taken from Microsoft Software License
> agreements (and Microsoft reseller / Managed Service Provider
> (MSP) channel papers when the instrument is a channel agreement).
> Do not invent product slang or coined classification labels.**

Microsoft is the channel reference
([`competitor-default-decisions.mdc`](competitor-default-decisions.mdc)).
This Cursor rule file may name Microsoft. Website, operator-facing,
and signed contract bodies must not
([`no-competitor-names-in-website.mdc`](no-competitor-names-in-website.mdc)).
Use the terms. Do not name the vendor in the paper.

## Scope

Operative surface: `pam-platform-docs/docs/go-to-market/contracts/`
and any future End User License Agreement (EULA), MSP Agreement,
Reseller, Alliance, Original Equipment Manufacturer (OEM) schedule,
Non-Disclosure Agreement (NDA), or related schedule. Cursor rules
and `docs/dev` may discuss the terms. They must not introduce slang
that later lands in a signed instrument.

## Forbidden

Do not write coined labels that are not ordinary license or channel
English, including:

- "lane," "Software Compensation Lane," "Marketplace Influence
  lane," "resale lane," "fee lane," or "No lane is a sublicense."
- Any new umbrella invented to classify compensation
- Product or go-to-market slang treated as if it were a defined
  legal term

Prefer: License, sublicense, end user, Licensed Software, Product,
Reseller, distributor, OEM, Managed Service Provider (as a party
role already used), purchase order, invoice, fees, remittance,
seller of record (commercial sale). "Partner of Record" only when
that phrase is actual partner-program language and is already
defined in the paper.

Name the actual transaction: marketplace sale, Provider-invoiced
sale, resale of a Provider-Customer license, or no software
compensation. If a defined term is still required, use ordinary
English ("Transaction Type" or "sale") and define it once without
"lane." Write "No sublicense is granted." or "This transaction is
not a sublicense." Do not write "No lane is a sublicense."

## Worked violation to fix

**Violation** (coined word used as if it were a contract term):

```text
No lane is a sublicense.
```

"Lane" is not a legal term commonly found in contracts.

**Fix** (standard license English):

```text
No sublicense is granted.
```

Or, where the sentence is about one sale:

```text
This transaction is not a sublicense.
```

## Required agent behavior

1. Before adding a label, ask whether a Microsoft Software License
   or Microsoft channel paper would use it. If not, use the
   ordinary license or commercial word.
2. Keep the pack architecture: two-party papers; Provider-Customer
   license in the EULA; no sublicense; MSP purchase order is
   MSP-to-Provider. Do not add Customer-requirement catalogs to
   partner papers. Do not insert LLM sidenotes
   ([`no-llm-sidenotes-in-contracts.mdc`](no-llm-sidenotes-in-contracts.mdc)).
3. Mirror this file to `core-assets/.cursor/rules/` in the same
   change set ([`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc)). The
   workspace `.cursor` directory is not a git root.

